From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] elf64-ppc: pad an odd-size .toc before the TOC edit

ppc64_elf_edit_toc drops unused TOC entries (those referenced only from
sections removed by --gc-sections) and shuffles the rest down, stepping
through a malloc'd copy of the section 8 bytes at a time and setting the
new size from where the step stopped.  ELFv1 and ELFv2 TOC entries are 8
bytes, so the size is always a multiple of 8.  CellOS Lv-2 ILP32 entries
are 4 bytes: a .toc with an odd number of them (a size of 4 mod 8) made
the last step read 4 bytes past the copy, and the rounded-up size wrote
those 4 heap bytes into the output .got.  The same objects linked from
two output paths of different lengths gave ELFs that differed in that
word ('dlPv', the tail of a freed "_ZdlPv" string, in one and zeros in
the other); nothing references the word, but links were not
byte-reproducible.

Fix: when the size is not a multiple of 8, grow the copy to the next
multiple and zero the pad before the shuffle.  The edited section keeps
the size it had (rounded up, as before); the pad word is now zero.

--- a/bfd/elf64-ppc.c
+++ b/bfd/elf64-ppc.c
@@ -9368,6 +9368,25 @@
 	  if (!bfd_malloc_and_get_section (ibfd, toc, &contents))
 	    goto error_ret;
 
+	  /* CellOS Lv-2 ILP32 .toc entries are 4 bytes, so the section
+	     size need not be a multiple of the 8-byte step below.  Pad
+	     the copy to one with zeros: the shuffle would otherwise read
+	     past the buffer, and the rounded-up size below would carry
+	     those heap bytes into the output.  */
+	  if ((toc->size & 7) != 0)
+	    {
+	      bfd_size_type padded = (toc->size + 7) & ~(bfd_size_type) 7;
+	      bfd_byte *grown = bfd_realloc (contents, padded);
+
+	      if (grown == NULL)
+		{
+		  free (contents);
+		  goto error_ret;
+		}
+	      memset (grown + toc->size, 0, padded - toc->size);
+	      contents = grown;
+	    }
+
 	  elf_section_data (toc)->this_hdr.contents = contents;
 
 	  for (src = contents, off = 0, drop = skip;
